      "corneringStiffnessN": 15000,
      "toeOutDeg": 2.0,
      "toeOutDegSource": "Standard 2 degrees of toe-out on every taildragger's mains (Ty 2026-11-10: \"Use 2 degrees toe out on all taildraggers standard unless I adjust based on feel\"). A standing slip angle outboard on each wheel: level the pair cancel, and when one is loaded more its outboard side force is the larger, pushing the aeroplane back toward the light side - \"The loaded tire tries to unload back to the other\". The sign comes from each wheel's own y, so one value means outboard on both (LandingGear.ToeRad). Live-tunable in OPTIONS while flying. THE P-51 WAS OFFERED A SMALLER NUMBER AND TURNED OUT NOT TO NEED ONE. Shown that 2 degrees broke CrosswindLandingFromTheLeft on the Mustang, Ty chose \"Less toe on the P-51\" (2026-11-10) and left the figure to whoever measured it. Measured: it was never the toe. At lane/claude-gear-flex (4805726a) the P-51 fails that test at 2.0 -- 19.9 m off centreline, wing tip 0.15 m, stab 0.08 m, propeller lost -- and at lane/claude-tv-companion (c882530d, \"Aircraft tyres are not car tyres: cornering stiffness to the published band, and the ground loop halves\") the SAME config passes it at 2.0: 8.0 m off centreline, tip 0.67 m, stab 0.37 m, rolled out to a taxi. The FLARE is identical in both (tip 1.46 m, 0.3 m off centreline), so the whole of the difference is in the rollout, which is what cornering stiffness governs; the excursion was a tyre artefact, not standing scrub. Still 8.0 m / 0.67 m after lane/claude-quest-islandnav was merged in (which raises TailwheelMuScale 0.5 -> 0.8), so the Mustang's rollout does not turn on that either. The sweep the number rests on, P-51 crosswind landing from the LEFT, worst tip clearance and distance off centreline at the end of the rollout: toe 0 -> 5.7 m / 0.69 m, 0.5 -> 6.3 / 0.66, 1.0 -> 7.2 / 0.65, 2.0 -> 8.0 / 0.67, 3.0 -> 6.8 / 0.76. Every one of them passes, and the tip margin is flat to within 0.11 m across the whole range: what 2 degrees COSTS this aeroplane is 2.3 m of centreline tracking (5.7 m at no toe, 8.0 m at 2) and no wingtip clearance whatever, and the wingtip is the thing the test is about -- which is why the Mustang keeps the fleet's 2 degrees rather than the 0.5 it was going to be cut to. REJECTED: cutting the Mustang to 0.5 (where the experiment had been left). It would have been tuned against a flight model that no longer exists and would have spent Ty's standard for nothing. NOT TOUCHED: the test's crosswind. 18.7 kt is 0.2 Vso of a 93.3 kt Vso -- 14 CFR 23.233(a) applied to a Mustang, not a published Mustang figure -- and Ty was offered \"argue with the crosswind figure\" and did not take it, so it stands as written.",
      "tire": "legacy-p51-main",
